This is the function the editor runs when calling a common event:
Game_Interpreter.prototype.command117 = function(params) {
const commonEvent = $dataCommonEvents[params[0]];
if (commonEvent) {
const eventId = this.isOnCurrentMap() ? this._eventId : 0;
this.setupChild(commonEvent.list, eventId);
}
return true;
};
rmmz_objects.js:10071 (on 1.0.2)
It picks the common event from the list, then makes sure it's not empty, and if the player is not transitioning maps for example, and then it adds it to the list of executing events in the map. You basically need to make sure to run it under the same conditions for it to work the same.
Then, for the other question, the labeling function is part of the event itself, so we only get a "true" value from running it as a script. There seems to be some kind of per event page array of labels, each defined as
{
"code": 118,
"indent": 0,
"parameters": [
"This is a label"
]
}
So you'd have to dynamically edit your $dataMap and reload your $gameMap every single time you add one. That, or simply sort and group the code in functions and run them in proper order.

There's general concensus on a global variable, "Imported" to set if a plugin has been already read or not by the editor. It's used for plugin extensions.
var Imported = Imported || {};
Imported.myPlugin = true;
There are many other ways to find it, but it's the easiest to do it that way.
If there are more global variables set or modified in the first plugin, you can use them after checking they are there.
For example...
if (Imported.myPlugin)
{
//Do stuff
}
or
if ($gameParty.parameterAddedOnAPluginSetAboveThis)
{
//Do stuff
}
